    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. We also deal with some patients who have specific neurological issues or substances in their system that make them act very \"small\" or \"skittish\"—lots of sudden, jerky movements and a tendency to hide or scurry away when we approach. It's a very different vibe from the loud, aggressive ones. Is there a way to tailor the de-escalation approach for these types of patients who seem to be acting in a more stunted or frantic way?",
